  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Frost or freezing temperatures are possible in \nmany areas tonight and Sunday night.\n\nWith a large area of Canadian high pressure building overhead\nthrough the weekend, conditions are favorable for radiational \ncooling (light to calm winds, mainly clear skies and a cool \nbackground airmass). At the moment, it is unclear which night \nwill be the coldest, although tonight (Saturday night) will have\na slight advantage given the position of the high overhead.\n\nThe growing season for 2026 has ended along and west of the Blue\nRidge and Catoctin Mountains, but continues east of there. Any\nheadlines for frost/freeze conditions will only be issued where\nthe growing season remains active.\n\nFor tonight, more widespread cold (possible frost/freeze) \nconditions are possible as high pressure settles more directly \noverhead. This would bring the frost/freeze threat for most \nareas outside the major urban centers (downtown DC/Baltimore) and\nimmediate shore of the Chesapeake Bay. If the colder guidance \nverifies, then the growing season may end for more areas west of \nI-95 come early Sunday morning. The high will remain overhead \nthrough Monday morning, and while the low-level airmass doesn't\nchange appreciably, temperatures will start warming aloft.\nTherefore frost/freeze coverage may be more limited Sunday\nnight, but still a risk in low lying areas.\n\nDid not make appreciable changes to the previous min temperature\nforecasts, which are near or below the coldest guidance which \nperforms best in radiational cooling (MOS, NBM 10 percentile and\nCanadian Regional).\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather continues through next week along\nwith generally seasonable temperatures.\n\nCanadian high pressure will continue to prevail overhead over the \nnext several days as upper-level ridging builds in from the west. As \nthis ridging tracks east, a dry backdoor cold front will move \nthrough Tuesday and into Wednesday, with winds shifting \nnortheasterly in the wake of the front. With PWATs remaining low \nthrough this period, no precipitation is anticipated as this front \nmoves through.\n\nAs this surface high pressure continues on, temperatures remain \nseasonable, with highs in the 60s to low 70s. Overnight lows could \ngradually increase somewhat throughout the week as this high \npressure persists, with skies remaining mostly clear throughout the \nweek. A few models hint at this high pressure breaking down towards \nthe end of next week as a weak trough begins to form over the Ohio \nRiver Valley next Thursday. Regardless, expect continued dry and \nseasonable conditions to continue at least through the next \nseveral days as the current pattern remains largely unchanged.",
